Given \(f(x)=\sqrt{x-2}\) and \(g(x)=x^{2}+1\),
\begin{itemize}
\item[a)] determine \(g(f(x))\).
\item[b)] explain why the domain of \(g(f(x))\) is restricted.
\end{itemize}

\section*{Solution}
\begin{itemize}
\item[a)]
\[
\begin{aligned}
g(f(x)) & =(\sqrt{x-2})^{2}+1 & & 1 \text { mark for composition } \\
& =x-2+1, x \geq 2 & & \mathbf{1} \text { mark } \\
& =x-1, x \geq 2 & &
\end{aligned}
\]
\item[b)] The domain of \(g(f(x))\) must be restricted because the domain of \(f(x)\) is restricted.
or
The value of the radicand must be positive.
1 mark
\end{itemize}

Solve algebraically.
\[
2 \log _{a} 3+\log _{a} 4=2, \text { where } a>0
\]

\section*{Solution}
\[
\begin{aligned}
\log _{a}\left(3^{2} \cdot 4\right) & =2 \\
\log _{a} 36 & =2 \\
a^{2} & =36 \\
a & =6
\end{aligned}
\]
